oy of payments-api v5.1.0 left the service degraded in all production regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west), with an 18% error rate. Mitigation: rolled back to v5.0.0, the most recent release that was healthy in production. The rollback is complete and verified in all regions. Next steps: confirm error rates have recovered and find the root cause in v5.1.0.", "idempotency_key": "payments-api-v5.1.0-rollback-sev2", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "Let me check that the service has recovered.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_0112cANCHzn1S2kyiEdg8bQJ", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments back to its last healthy release and opened a SEV2 incident. The error rate has dropped from 18% to 0.2%, and the service is reported healthy again.\n\n1. **Found the service:** There's no service called \"payments\", so I tried likely names. The real one is `payments-api`. It was running v5.1.0 in us-east, us-west and eu-west, marked unhealthy with an 18% error rate.\n2. **Picked the version:** The release history shows v5.0.0 (healthy in production) and then v5.1.0 (degraded). So v5.0.0 was the most recent healthy release.\n3. **Rolled back:** `payments-api` is now on v5.0.0 in all three regions, and the system confirmed it.\n4. **Opened the incident:** **INC-0001** (SEV2, `payments-api`) is open and on-call has been paged. The summary covers what went wrong, the rollback, and next steps: keep an eye on error rates and find the root cause of the problem in v5.1.0.\n\nNo approval was needed because v5.0.0 has been healthy in production before.", "type": "text"}]}]}